# Former Gods, Part 12
Tamil title: மாஜி கடவுள்கள் - பகுதி 12

Animal gods too would spring upon the soul, bite and torment it, and drive it to hell.

If it obtained the “fortune” of reaching heaven, however, there would be no shortage of comfort. Each person would receive an allotted tract of land and could cultivate it for a livelihood. Plenty prevailed, so famine never appeared. If a resident of heaven wished to see his people on earth, the soul could assume the form of a bird, visit them, and return.

Such was Egyptian mythology. Did it lack imagination? Yet are Egyptians today destroying themselves by believing it?

Nandi and Narada remain objects of worship here. There, gods with jackal and lion faces were once divine but are now former gods. Our puranas contain a god in bovine form and one with a horse’s face, and we still revile anyone who disbelieves them as an atheist, despite knowing how far reason has spread throughout the world. In Egypt, when deceivers controlled the ignorant, ram-faced gods and frog-faced goddesses sat enthroned in temples. Today Egyptians would clap and laugh at even speaking of “gods” in the plural. Ra, Osiris, Isis, and the others became former gods when reason triumphed. Here, even Irulandi refuses to become former; ignorance still wields such influence.

## The God Who Lost His Arm

“Do not come! Do not come!”

“Go away! Go away!”

“Get out of the house!”

That was the welcome received when approaching one god’s mansion. Three cranes stood at its entrance and hurled these harsh words at visitors. Seeing anyone approach, the first cried, “Do not come!” If the visitor ignored it and stood before the mansion, the second shouted, “Go away!” If the visitor still remained, the third drove him off with, “Get out of the house!”

A god employed these remarkable cranes to repel visitors and dwelt there in divine state, not among Eskimos, but long ago in Britain, famed for building an empire upon which the sun never set, before reason had triumphed.

Britain, like other lands, practised polytheism. Each “divine form” possessed a peculiar disposition. Seeking their grace, Britons worshipped all the deities, built temples, held magnificent festivals, offered many sacrifices, and observed varied rites.

A priestly class explained which god required which worship, when it must be performed, what benefit followed, and what fate awaited neglect. It issued commands and received offerings. Britons could not see the gods’ power directly, but they saw the priests’ power and trembled.

The priests who controlled ordinary people in ancient Britain were called Druids. People performed penance for a Druid’s smile; if he frowned, they lamented that their fate was ruined. Druids possessed such influence. People believed only they could stand between gods and humans, obtain grace, or bring down curses. Their word was honoured above a king’s law. Failure to pay the king’s tax might bring prison; failure to offer the priest a gift brought hell. Hell! The thought itself set the heart afire. Thus ordinary people lay enslaved to Druids.

“A human sacrifice is required to appease this god,” the Druid commanded, and a man was cut down at the altar. “The Mother asks a thousand goats,” he said, and people drove flocks to the temple.

“That beautiful woman...?” asked the Druid.

“An offering,” the people replied submissively. The Druid was sacred, blessed by God, part divine.

He was not merely priest but teacher, jurist, historian, scholar of every science, magician, and physician. The thunder god came running when he called; the sky god granted every boon he requested. So people believed, and the priesthood gained immense power.

A Druid paid no tax. How could a mere king tax God’s representative? When war began, farmer, woodcutter, wealthy man, and shopkeeper took up swords; the Druid did not enter battle but went to the temple with his rosary. He could expel anyone from society.

Once knowledge grew, not a trace remained of the Druids who had exercised such domination. People had mistaken priestly lies for sacred speech and regarded serving Druids as holy duty. With reason’s aid, they recognized those words as fraud. They had offered gifts believing they passed through the priest to God and obeyed every command. We still see that same condition here.

Novel light has entered every other sphere, while religion remains dark. When some courageously tear at that darkness, not only ordinary people but cunning “intellectuals” determined to keep them ordinary arise to condemn the reformers as atheists and enemies of religion.

For ages Britain believed Druids divine messengers and danced to their direction. As knowledge grew, their authority shook. When reason’s storm blew, it fell like a great tree with destroyed roots. Today Druid power is not even a grandmother’s tale to British grandchildren. Here, a rational thinker can only grieve. People still say a holy man’s touch makes a dead tree sprout, his foot turns desert into garden, and drinking water that washed his feet gives a barren woman a jewel-like child. Deceivers respond, “Indeed! Can miracles cease? Could the world survive without incarnate men?”

A poet says the world lies in a dark chamber after seeing this country. Darkness existed elsewhere too and once filled Britain. Ordinary Britons suffered under Druids, rulers of that darkness; knowledge freed them. Here knowledge meets prison, prohibition, punishment, and condemnation. Druids have not disappeared. Priestly power survives.

The priest points to heaven and deceives the poor, tells puranas and exploits ordinary people: “This god grants boons: one bunch of bananas, twenty-five coconuts, and eight rupees as fee.” The hut-dweller gives everything. “My belly is cool,” says the priest; “my disease worsens,” says blind Kuppan. Druids vanished there because reason denied them room. Here they employ a thousand and eight devices to keep reason out. Puranic falsehood still has space.

Britain and other lands where knowledge triumphed no longer speak of many gods. During Druid rule, believers accepted many gods and doubters were sinners. The myth of the god guarded by three cranes entered minds in that age. No one worships those cranes now. Their divinity disappeared with the priestly authority that manufactured it.

Another god, Midir, possessed a wife who was abducted. One cannot even ask the god to recover her, because her abductor was Aengus, another powerful, boon-granting god, and Midir’s own brother. What can devotees say? Whom can they condemn? The deed was among the most heinous sins, but its perpetrator was a god. Today many might ask: “Can divinity possess such character? Such baseness belongs to the worst humans, whom society condemns and punishes. Would God act so?” In Druid Britain no such “sinners” existed. Devotees worshipped even Aengus, regarding the theft as another divine sport.

Unreasoning Britain possessed whole families of gods. The primal deity was Mother Danu and her consort Bile. Next came Dagda, the pleasant-faced god. He owned a marvellous vessel that supplied food according to each person’s virtue. Hunter, labourer, thief, trader, herald, and field worker imagined they lived not by their labour but upon food from Dagda’s vessel. Its supply grew if an offering pleased the Druid and diminished if his heart remained cold. Such thought controlled the people.

Dagda wore a brown horsehide garment and played a wooden harp whose music produced the seasons. He possessed a huge mace for destroying enemies, so large it had to be hauled on a cart. He defeated the monster Mata, with four heads and a thousand legs. This god had an enormous appetite for porridge.

Even now our temples publish menus of each god’s favourite offering: sweet rice for Ranganatha, pepper vadai for Venkateswara, thenkuzhal and idli for Varadaraja, large vadai for Nataraja, appam, flattened rice, and puffed grain for Vinayaka. Likewise, in Britain’s unenlightened age, priests said Dagda loved porridge, and devotees believed them.

Dagda and the goddess Boann had five children: Brigid, Aengus, Midir, Ogma, and Bodb, just as Vinayaka and Murugan were born on Kailasa.

Aengus was handsome and possessed a golden harp; his kisses reportedly became birds. He was the god who abducted the wife of his brother Midir.

Ogma was honey-tongued, and those receiving his grace became poets.

The youngest, Bodb, ruled after Dagda. He bore the epithet “the red one.” Here people sing of Siva’s golden body and Vishnu’s dark one; when Britain lay in darkness, they worshipped Bodb as red-bodied. He possessed yellow and red spears; swords called Avenger, Great Fury, and Little Fury; a boat called Wave-Sweeper; a horse that travelled on land and water; magic armour; and a magic sword. He also kept many excellent pigs.

Krishna herded cattle; Britain’s Lord herded pigs. Bodb frequently entertained the gods with perfectly cooked pork. That was not the only wonder: after the pigs were killed, cooked, and eaten, they came alive again.

Bodb lived with his goddess Morrigu and was worshipped. A son was born to them, and the divine world panicked. The medical god declared that the child must be killed or heaven and earth would perish. He killed the divine child and split open its heart, finding three snakes. Saying they would destroy both worlds if allowed to grow, he killed and burned them. When their ashes were dissolved in a river, it reportedly became a river of fire.

Druids invented such myths; people believed them, poured out offerings, and built temples. Where are those temples? On wooded British hills, people now point to certain stones and say they are temples of the Druid age.

Druids ruled across Britain, Ireland, France, and neighbouring countries, frightening and enslaving ordinary minds with meaningless tales. It took a long time for people to ask whether those frauds lacked all sense and contained no idea for human advancement. Even the thought that the Druid, not Dagda, drank the porridge was afraid to appear.

Slowly but continuously knowledge spread. People began thinking, investigating, and demanding causes and explanations. They recognized priestly sacred stories as lies, though for a time lacked courage to say so aloud. A few eventually spoke and suffered greatly. Some endured religious tyranny and declared they would reveal truth even at the cost of life.

Druids roared and ordered rulers to behead the wretches insulting the noble religion. Heads fell. The first rank of rationalists became corpses, but second, third, and ever-new ranks advanced. The executioners tired; ordinary people gained clarity; rationalist arrows flew faster and tore away religious coverings. Druids panicked and enlisted armies, crowned rulers, and palace-dwellers, without success.

“Show cause or close your shop!” rationalists cried. What cause could be shown for one god stealing another’s wife or world-destroying snakes inhabiting a child’s heart? The Druid knew his age had ended and lost authority. When the priest departed, could his imaginary figures remain? Dagda, Midir, Aengus, Bile, and the whole divine company vanished and became former gods. Once they controlled five or six countries; enlightenment made them former gods.

They had possessed more influence than Kadan, Madan, Katteri, Irulan, Idumban, and Ekatha still possess here. Yet Dagda, Midir, Aengus, and the rest became former gods when clarity arose in Britain.

Such stories circulated abundantly when Druid priests dominated Britain and Ireland.

While Nuada ruled as lord of heaven, a demonic host attacked the gods and cut off the god’s arm.

Yes, sir, his arm. Do you laugh? Learning that Britain once held such madness makes one clap and laugh. But this insane divine legend belonged to Britain’s fog-bound age of blind knowledge, not today. Its people abandoned it centuries ago.

We still say it is sinful even to call a story the tale of Siva plucking off insolent Brahma’s head. We worship him as Kapali and sing praise of the beheading. Devotees would burn with their eyes anyone asking whether this is a god’s deed. Britain sent the one-armed god’s story to the rubbish heap; here “scholars” call only believers in the headless Brahma story theists.

Nuada’s tale was invented when British ordinary people suffered the same madness still present among ours. A medical god immediately gave the amputee a wondrous silver arm that functioned like a real one. But the divine assembly decided that a maimed god should not reign, removed Nuada from the throne, and selected another.

Many gods ruled the sea world. Their primal god was Elatha. They selected his son Bres to sit upon the throne and rule. Bres was also allowed to marry Brigid, daughter of Dagda.

The old god lost arm and office. Bres ascended as the new god beside Brigid. This is news from the divine world, not a tale of forest kings.

The new ruler favoured inhabitants of the sea world and oppressed his new subjects with unbearable taxes and unspeakable suffering. Old gods were struck by famine and forced to labour to the bone. Even Dagda had to build fortifications to survive. You may mockingly ask whether a god built forts. People wandering in ignorance believed it then; here today we hear scholars broadcast in sweet Tamil the episode of God carrying earth for pittu.

The gods groaned under tyranny while Bres controlled the new realm.

Then Miach, son of the medical god Dian Cecht, used magical medicine to recover Nuada’s severed arm from its burial pit and attach it. Nuada regained his lost arm and everyone rejoiced, except one god: Miach’s father. Jealous that his son surpassed him, Dian Cecht summoned Miach and smashed his head with a hammer. Three times Miach’s medicine saved him. The fourth blow split his skull and brain into two, and the god Miach died. At the place where they buried him...

